-- Q10816: C Corporations [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["NOL carryforwards transfer automatically and cannot be challenged by the IRS after acquisition","The IRS may disallow the NOL deductions under Section 269 if the principal purpose was to obtain a tax benefit","The IRS can challenge only when the acquisition price exceeds fair market value of net assets","Section 269 applies exclusively to asset acquisitions and does not cover stock purchases"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 269(a), if any person acquires control (50%+) of a corporation and the principal purpose is to obtain a deduction or allowance not otherwise available, the IRS may disallow such benefits. Westbrook''s documented intent to use Thornton''s NOLs with minimal business purpose supports disallowance.
Wrong (A): While NOLs technically survive an acquisition, Section 269 provides independent authority to disallow tax benefits when the acquisition lacks genuine business purpose.
Wrong (C): Section 269 does not require the price to exceed FMV; it focuses on the principal purpose of the acquisition being to obtain a tax benefit.
Wrong (D): Section 269(a) applies to acquisitions of stock control as well as asset acquisitions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10816;

-- Q10832: Partnerships [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The partner''s proportionate share of total partnership liabilities","The partner''s personal bank account designated for partnership transactions","A record tracking a partner''s equity based on contributions, distributions, and allocations","The cumulative cash contributions the partner has made over time"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.704-1(b)(2)(iv), a partner''s capital account tracks their equity stake in the partnership. It is increased by contributions and allocated income and decreased by distributions and allocated losses.
Wrong (A): A partner''s share of liabilities affects outside basis under Section 752 but is not part of the capital account.
Wrong (B): A capital account is an internal partnership record, not an external bank account used for transactions.
Wrong (D): Capital accounts reflect more than just cash contributions; they also include property contributions, income allocations, and reductions for distributions and losses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10832;

-- Q10843: Partnerships [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The excess loss is permanently disallowed and cannot be used in future years","The excess is reported as a deduction on the partnership''s Form 1065 return","The excess is suspended and carried forward until Chambers has sufficient basis","The excess loss automatically converts to a capital loss carryforward"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 704(d), a partner cannot deduct losses exceeding their outside basis. The $15,000 excess is suspended and carried forward indefinitely until Chambers''s basis increases through income allocations, contributions, or increased liability share.
Wrong (A): The loss is not permanently disallowed; Section 704(d) suspends the excess with no expiration period for basis-limited partnership losses.
Wrong (B): The partnership reports the full $35,000 on Form 1065, but the limitation applies at the individual partner level on the partner''s return.
Wrong (D): Suspended partnership losses retain their character as ordinary losses; they do not convert to capital losses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10843;

-- Q10846: Partnerships [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Dawson must contribute $15,000 in cash to restore the capital account deficit","The partnership must allocate the first $15,000 of income to Dawson to eliminate the deficit","The deficit is forgiven automatically and requires no further corrective action","The partnership must distribute $15,000 to other partners to offset Dawson''s deficit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.704-1(b)(2)(ii)(d), a qualified income offset requires the partnership to allocate items of income and gain to a partner with an unexpected capital account deficit as quickly as possible to eliminate it. This serves as an alternative to a deficit restoration obligation.
Wrong (A): A qualified income offset provision specifically avoids requiring cash contributions; income allocations restore the deficit instead.
Wrong (C): The deficit is not forgiven; without either a deficit restoration obligation or a qualified income offset, allocations creating the deficit would lack economic effect.
Wrong (D): Distributions to other partners do not eliminate a specific partner''s capital account deficit; only income allocations to Dawson address the negative balance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10846;

-- Q10850: Partnerships [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a guaranteed payment under Section 707(c), subject to self-employment tax","As a distribution from the partnership that reduces Quinn''s outside basis","As a tax-free fringe benefit excludable from Quinn''s gross income","As a Section 707(a) payment to a non-partner, deductible by the partnership"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 707(a)(1), when a partner transacts with the partnership in a capacity other than as a partner at arm''s-length rates, the transaction is treated as between the partnership and a third party. Quinn billed at standard rates and the payment was not tied to partnership income.
Wrong (A): Guaranteed payments under Section 707(c) are for services rendered in a partner capacity and are determined without regard to partnership income; Quinn acted as an independent service provider.
Wrong (B): A distribution reduces outside basis, but this arm''s-length service transaction is not characterized as a distribution under partnership rules.
Wrong (C): Payments to partners for services are not excludable fringe benefits; they must be reported as income by the recipient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10850;

-- Q10856: Partnerships [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$130,000 under Section 736(b) for assets, $30,000 under 736(a) for unstated goodwill, $90,000 under 736(a) for services","$250,000 entirely classified under Section 736(b) as payments for partnership property","$160,000 under Section 736(b) for property and $90,000 under Section 736(a) for services","$250,000 entirely classified under Section 736(a) as a distributive share of income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 736(b)(2)(B), for a general partnership, payments for goodwill are classified under Section 736(b) only if the partnership agreement specifically provides for goodwill payments. Since the agreement is silent, the $30,000 goodwill falls under Section 736(a), the $130,000 for other assets is Section 736(b), and the $90,000 for services is Section 736(a).
Wrong (B): This incorrectly classifies all payments as property payments, ignoring the service component and the special goodwill rule for general partnerships.
Wrong (C): This classification fails to separate the $30,000 goodwill from the $130,000 in other assets; unstated goodwill must be reclassified to Section 736(a).
Wrong (D): Section 736(b) property payments exist here for the $130,000 in non-goodwill assets; classifying everything as 736(a) ignores the property component.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10856;

-- Q10873: S Corporations [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Included in Delaney''s W-2 wages because >2% S corp shareholders are treated as partners","Tax-free qualified transportation fringe under Section 132(f) available to all employees","Deductible by Silverleaf Corp but excluded from Delaney''s taxable gross income","Excluded from gross income but separately subject to self-employment tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1372, an S corporation shareholder owning more than 2% of the stock is treated as a partner for fringe benefit purposes. Qualified transportation fringes under Section 132(f), including employer-paid parking, do not extend to partners or >2% S corp shareholders, so the $6,000 must be included in Delaney''s W-2.
Wrong (B): Section 1372 overrides the general fringe benefit exclusion for >2% shareholders; only rank-and-file employees qualify for the Section 132(f) exclusion.
Wrong (C): Because the benefit is includible in Delaney''s income, it cannot be excluded; Silverleaf may deduct it as compensation but Delaney must report it.
Wrong (D): The parking benefit is treated as W-2 wages, not as an item subject to self-employment tax on a separate schedule.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10873;

-- Q10907: Alternative Minimum Tax [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The AMT computation is unchanged because SALT is fully added back regardless of the cap","AMT liability increases because the SALT cap reduces regular tax more than AMT","The SALT cap reduces the AMT add-back from $35,000 to $10,000, narrowing the AMT gap","The TCJA eliminated AMT entirely for taxpayers residing in high-tax states"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(A), only the SALT deduction actually claimed on the regular return is added back for AMT. Pre-TCJA, Brennan would deduct and add back $35,000. Under TCJA, only $10,000 is deducted and added back, reducing the AMT adjustment by $25,000 and narrowing the gap between regular taxable income and AMTI.
Wrong (A): The AMT add-back equals the deduction claimed, not the amount of taxes paid; the cap directly reduces the add-back amount.
Wrong (B): The SALT cap actually narrows the regular-tax-to-AMT gap rather than increasing AMT, because a smaller deduction means a smaller add-back.
Wrong (D): The TCJA did not eliminate AMT; it raised exemption amounts and phaseout thresholds but kept AMT in effect for all taxpayers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10907;

-- Q10914: Alternative Minimum Tax [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Charitable contributions made to qualified tax-exempt organizations","Percentage depletion claimed in excess of the property''s adjusted basis","Interest expense incurred on primary residence acquisition indebtedness","Qualified business income deduction claimed under Section 199A"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 57(a)(1), percentage depletion in excess of the adjusted basis of the property is a tax preference item added to taxable income when computing AMTI. Unlike adjustments, preference items always increase AMTI.
Wrong (A): Charitable contributions are not an AMT preference item or adjustment; they are allowed in full under both regular tax and AMT.
Wrong (C): Acquisition indebtedness interest on a primary residence is deductible for both regular tax and AMT; it is not a preference item.
Wrong (D): The QBI deduction under Section 199A is allowed in computing both regular taxable income and AMTI and is not classified as an AMT preference item.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10914;

-- Q10939: Individual Taxation: Credits [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Fully refundable, generating a refund if it exceeds tax liability","Nonrefundable, with unused amounts carried forward up to five years","Partially refundable at 40% of the unused credit amount","Nonrefundable with no carryforward of any unused portion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 23, the adoption credit is nonrefundable, meaning it can reduce tax liability to zero but cannot generate a refund. However, unused amounts may be carried forward for up to five years under Section 23(c).
Wrong (A): The adoption credit is explicitly nonrefundable, unlike credits such as the EIC or the refundable portion of the AOTC that can produce refunds.
Wrong (C): There is no 40% partial refundability provision for the adoption credit; it is entirely nonrefundable with only a carryforward mechanism.
Wrong (D): While the credit is nonrefundable, Section 23(c) specifically provides a five-year carryforward for any unused portion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10939;

-- Q10942: Individual Taxation: Credits [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$1,700, the maximum refundable ACTC per child for the 2026 tax year","$2,000, because the full unused CTC converts to a refundable credit","$3,375, computed as 15% of earned income in excess of $2,500","$800, the standard refundable amount designated for one qualifying child"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 24(d), the ACTC equals the lesser of the unused CTC or 15% of earned income above $2,500, capped at $1,700 per child. Franklin''s earned income formula yields 15% x ($25,000 - $2,500) = $3,375, but the refundable portion is capped at $1,700.
Wrong (B): The full $2,000 CTC is not entirely refundable; only up to $1,700 per child may be refunded as the ACTC.
Wrong (C): While 15% of excess earned income equals $3,375, the ACTC is capped at $1,700 per qualifying child.
Wrong (D): There is no $800 standard refundable amount; the ACTC is calculated using the earned income formula with a $1,700 per-child cap.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10942;

