-- Q10948: Individual Taxation: Credits [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$28,000, which is 100% of prior-year tax under the safe harbor rule","$30,800, which is 110% of prior-year tax under the higher-income threshold","$37,800, which is 90% of the current-year tax liability amount","$42,000, which is 100% of the current-year tax liability amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 6654(d)(1)(B), the prior-year safe harbor requires payment of 100% of the prior-year tax liability when prior-year AGI is $150,000 or less. Since Reed''s prior-year AGI of $140,000 is below the threshold, he must pay only $28,000 to avoid the penalty.
Wrong (B): The 110% safe harbor applies only when prior-year AGI exceeds $150,000; Reed''s AGI of $140,000 falls below that threshold.
Wrong (C): While 90% of current-year tax is an alternative safe harbor, it results in a higher required payment ($37,800) than the prior-year method ($28,000).
Wrong (D): The safe harbor does not require paying 100% of the current-year tax; that would eliminate any benefit of the estimated tax safe harbor rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10948;

-- Q10970: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A taxpayer who filed an extension for the current-year tax return","A taxpayer who claimed only the standard deduction on the return","A taxpayer who made at least one quarterly estimated tax payment","A taxpayer with zero prior-year tax liability who was a U.S. citizen all year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 6654(e)(2), no underpayment penalty applies if the taxpayer had zero tax liability in the prior year and was a U.S. citizen or resident for the entire year. This is a complete exemption regardless of the current-year tax owed.
Wrong (A): Filing an extension delays only the filing deadline, not the payment obligation; it does not exempt a taxpayer from the underpayment penalty.
Wrong (B): Claiming the standard deduction has no bearing on the estimated tax penalty; the exemption depends on prior-year tax liability, not deduction method.
Wrong (C): Making one quarterly payment does not satisfy the safe harbor unless total payments meet the required threshold under Section 6654(d).$EXPL$
