-- Q11008: Individual Taxation: Deductions [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["AGI minus the greater of standard or itemized deductions, minus QBI deduction if applicable","Gross income minus itemized deductions only, without considering standard deduction","AGI minus above-the-line deductions, which are subtracted a second time","Gross income minus the standard deduction and then minus all applicable tax credits"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sections 63 and 199A, taxable income equals AGI minus the greater of the standard deduction or itemized deductions, minus the qualified business income deduction if applicable. This formula sequence (gross income to AGI to taxable income) is fundamental.
Wrong (B): Taxpayers may choose either the standard deduction or itemized deductions; this formula omits the standard deduction option entirely.
Wrong (C): Above-the-line deductions are already subtracted to arrive at AGI and are not deducted again in computing taxable income.
Wrong (D): Tax credits reduce tax liability, not taxable income; they are applied after the tax is computed on taxable income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11008;

-- Q11009: Individual Taxation: Deductions [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Alternative tax, Credits, Insurance premiums, Depreciation recapture","Alimony (pre-2019), IRA contributions, Student loan interest, 50% SE tax","Adjusted income, Capital losses, Itemized deductions, Dividends received","Accounting fees, Charitable gifts, Investment expenses, Professional dues"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 62(a), above-the-line deductions reduce gross income to AGI. The ACID mnemonic captures Alimony paid (Section 215, pre-2019 agreements), Contributions to IRA (Section 219), Interest on student loans (Section 221), and Deduction for 50% of self-employment tax (Section 164(f)).
Wrong (A): Alternative tax and credits are not above-the-line deductions; these terms do not correspond to Section 62(a) adjustments.
Wrong (C): Capital losses and itemized deductions are not above-the-line deductions; itemized deductions are below-the-line and reduce AGI to taxable income.
Wrong (D): Accounting fees, charitable gifts, and professional dues are not above-the-line deductions under current law; charitable contributions are itemized deductions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11009;

-- Q11022: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because the joint return exception applies when filed solely to claim a refund","No, because a married dependent filing jointly can never be claimed by another taxpayer","Yes, but only if Kendra''s gross income falls below the $5,050 threshold","No, because marriage automatically terminates a parent''s right to claim a dependent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 152(b)(2), a dependent generally cannot file a joint return. However, an exception applies when the joint return is filed only to claim a refund and neither spouse has a tax liability filing separately. Kendra and Luis meet both conditions.
Wrong (B): The joint return prohibition has an exception; it is not an absolute bar when the return is filed solely to recover withheld taxes.
Wrong (C): The $5,050 gross income test applies to the qualifying relative rules, not to the joint return exception under Section 152(b)(2).
Wrong (D): Marriage does not automatically terminate dependency status; the joint return test and its exception govern whether a married dependent can still be claimed.$EXPL$
