-- Q11145: S Corporations [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Archer''s S election automatically terminates because S corporations cannot hold accumulated E&P","Archer inherits the $200,000 E&P under Section 381, creating Section 1375 tax exposure","The $200,000 E&P is eliminated upon acquisition because the acquirer is an S corporation","Archer inherits the E&P but it is immediately distributed as a tax-free dividend"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 381(c)(2), the acquiring corporation in a tax-free reorganization inherits the target''s accumulated E&P. An S corporation can carry accumulated E&P from acquisitions, exposing Archer to the Section 1375 excess net passive income tax and the three-year termination rule under Section 1362(d)(3).
Wrong (A): Inheriting E&P does not itself terminate the S election; it merely creates exposure to the passive income tax and potential future termination.
Wrong (C): E&P is not eliminated upon acquisition; Section 381 requires the acquiring corporation to inherit the target''s accumulated E&P regardless of S corporation status.
Wrong (D): There is no automatic distribution of inherited E&P; it remains on the corporate books and affects the AAA ordering rules for future distributions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11145;

-- Q11190: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Lost profits are automatically recoverable as direct damages in any breach of contract","Lost profits are never recoverable because they are inherently speculative in nature","Lost profits are recoverable only if Summit had reason to know of the resale at contracting","Lost profits are recoverable regardless of foreseeability if Baxter proves the exact amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Hadley v. Baxendale, codified in Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 351, consequential damages such as lost profits are recoverable only if reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party at contract formation. Because the contract was silent about the resale, Baxter must prove Summit knew or had reason to know of it.
Wrong (A): Lost profits are consequential, not direct, damages; they require a showing of foreseeability beyond what naturally flows from the breach itself.
Wrong (B): Lost profits are not categorically barred; they are recoverable when the breaching party could reasonably foresee them at the time of contracting.
Wrong (D): Proving the exact amount alone is insufficient; the foreseeability requirement under Hadley v. Baxendale must also be satisfied.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11190;

-- Q11194: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only if the actual damages suffered by Greer exceed the stipulated liquidated amount","If the amount was a reasonable estimate of harm and actual damages were difficult to calculate","If the breaching party agreed to the clause, regardless of whether the amount is reasonable","Only if the non-breaching party proves it suffered damages exactly equal to $30,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 356, a liquidated damages clause is enforceable when two conditions are met: the amount was a reasonable forecast of anticipated harm at the time of contracting, and actual damages were difficult to estimate precisely. Reasonableness is judged at formation.
Wrong (A): Enforceability does not require actual damages to exceed the liquidated amount; the clause is judged by its reasonableness at the time of contracting.
Wrong (C): Mutual agreement alone is insufficient; a clause imposing a disproportionately large amount unrelated to anticipated harm is an unenforceable penalty.
Wrong (D): The non-breaching party does not need to prove exact damages equaling the liquidated amount; the purpose of liquidated damages is to avoid that proof requirement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11194;

-- Q11195: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Newton''s modification is enforceable under UCC 2-209; Archer''s fails under common law","Both modifications are enforceable because both parties voluntarily agreed to the changes","Neither modification is enforceable because both lack the required new consideration","Newton''s modification fails as oral; Archer''s is enforceable because services avoid UCC"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-209(1), a contract for the sale of goods may be modified without new consideration if made in good faith. Newton''s steel shortage provides a legitimate commercial reason. Under common law, Archer''s promise to perform the same design work for more money violates the pre-existing duty rule.
Wrong (B): Common law requires new consideration for modifications; Archer''s agreement to pay more for the same services is unenforceable without additional consideration.
Wrong (C): The UCC eliminates the consideration requirement for good-faith modifications of goods contracts; Newton''s modification does not need new consideration.
Wrong (D): The issue is not oral versus written form; the key distinction is that the UCC relaxes the consideration requirement for goods while common law retains it for services.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11195;

-- Q11197: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A promise made under duress that is voidable at the election of the aggrieved party","A promise to perform an act that is illegal or contrary to established public policy","A promise made without the knowledge or awareness of the other contracting party","A statement appearing to be a promise that imposes no actual obligation on the promisor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 71, consideration requires a bargained-for exchange in which each party incurs a legal detriment. An illusory promise imposes no actual obligation on the promisor and therefore cannot serve as valid consideration, rendering the agreement unenforceable.
Wrong (A): A voidable promise made under duress is binding until rescinded; it differs from an illusory promise, which fails at formation due to lack of commitment.
Wrong (B): A promise to perform an illegal act concerns legality of the contract''s subject matter, not whether the promisor has made a binding commitment.
Wrong (C): Lack of knowledge relates to mutual assent issues, not to the concept of illusory promises, which concern the absence of any binding obligation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11197;
